---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="UOT-DETR | Amirhossein Eskorouchi"
  description="UOT-DETR is a distributionally constrained visual quality measurement framework that integrates unbalanced optimal transport into DETR training for wood-chip size estimation."
>
  <article class="page project-detail-page">
    <header class="page-header project-detail-header">
      <p class="project-detail-category">
        Machine Learning · Optimization · Intelligent Manufacturing
      </p>

      <h1 class="page-title">
        Distributionally Constrained Visual Quality Measurement Using
        Unbalanced Optimal Transport
      </h1>

      <p class="project-detail-subtitle">
        Wood-chip size estimation for manufacturing process monitoring
      </p>

      <p class="project-detail-authors">
        <strong>Amirhossein Eskorouchi</strong>, Jason T. Street,
        Haifeng Wang, and Mohammad Marufuzzaman
      </p>

      <p class="project-detail-status">
        Under review at <em>Decision Support Systems</em>
      </p>

      <p class="research-entry-links" aria-label="UOT-DETR resources">
        <a
          href="https://github.com/amirhossein-eskorouchi/UOT-DETR"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >
          Code
        </a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
        <a
          href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18392693"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >
          Dataset
        </a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <figure class="project-hero-figure">
      <a
        href="/images/projects/uot-detr/uot_detr_architecture.png"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noreferrer"
        aria-label="Open the full-resolution UOT-DETR architecture figure"
      >
        <img
          src="/images/projects/uot-detr/uot_detr_architecture.png"
          alt="Architecture of UOT-DETR showing the baseline DETR pipeline and unbalanced optimal transport regularizer"
          width="2630"
          height="1545"
        />
      </a>

      <figcaption>
        UOT-DETR adds a training-time population-level objective to the
        standard DETR pipeline while preserving the original inference path.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Research problem</h2>

      <p>
        Conventional object detectors are optimized to identify individual
        objects accurately. In manufacturing quality monitoring, however, the
        downstream decision may depend on recovering the physical distribution
        of the complete object population. Missed small, overlapping, or
        partially occluded chips can therefore bias the estimated size
        distribution even when conventional detection metrics appear strong.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Method</h2>

      <p>
        UOT-DETR integrates an unbalanced optimal transport regularizer into
        DETR training. The regularizer compares predicted and reference measures
        in normalized log-size space and accommodates missing or additional
        predicted mass. It complements DETR's instance-level Hungarian
        supervision with a population-level distribution objective.
      </p>

      <p>
        The UOT component is used only during training. Prediction follows the
        standard DETR inference path, so the method introduces no additional
        inference-time overhead.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Selected results</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>mAR50: 95</li>
        <li>Overall mAR50-95: 72</li>
        <li>Small-object mAR50-95: 42</li>
        <li>Small-object mAR50 improvement over DETR: 24.7 percentage points</li>
        <li>Small-object mAP50 improvement over DETR: 15.4 percentage points</li>
        <li>Reported KL divergence: 0.041 to 0.048</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The largest improvements occurred for small objects in medium- and
        high-clutter scenes, where faithful recovery is most difficult and most
        relevant to population-level quality measurement.
      </p>
    </section>

